🚀 Day 17 of #DrGViswanathanChallenge

Today, I solved LeetCode 560 – Subarray Sum Equals K.

This problem introduced me to one of the most useful DSA techniques for array problems: Prefix Sum + HashMap. Instead of checking every possible subarray, I learned how previously computed prefix sums can be reused to count valid subarrays efficiently.

💡 Approach: Prefix Sum + HashMap

I first computed the prefix sum of the array, where each element stores the cumulative sum up to that index.

Then, while traversing the prefix sum array:

If the current prefix sum itself equals k, I found one valid subarray.
I searched for (currentPrefixSum - k) in a HashMap.
If it existed, every occurrence of that prefix sum represented another valid subarray ending at the current index.
The HashMap stored the frequency of each prefix sum, allowing me to count multiple subarrays in constant time.

This reduced the brute-force O(n²) solution to an optimized O(n) approach with O(n) extra space.

📚 What I Learned:

✅ Using Prefix Sum to represent cumulative sums
✅ Leveraging a HashMap to store prefix sum frequencies
✅ Counting multiple subarrays efficiently using frequencies
✅ Transforming a quadratic solution into a linear one
✅ Recognizing another important interview pattern for array problems

